<ToolLayout title="SaaS Runway Calculator — How to Calculate Startup Runway" description="Calculate your monthly burn rate and how many months of runway you have left, using net or gross burn.">
  <RunwayCalculator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Net Burn vs Gross Burn: Don't Mix Them Up</h2>
  <p><strong>Gross burn</strong> is total monthly expenses, ignoring revenue. <strong>Net burn</strong> is expenses minus revenue — the actual rate cash is depleting. Runway should always be calculated from net burn; using gross burn understates how long you actually have, since it ignores money coming in.</p>
  <p><strong>Net Burn = Monthly Expenses − Monthly Revenue.</strong> If revenue exceeds expenses, net burn is negative — meaning you're not burning cash at all, you're extending runway indefinitely (assuming that holds).</p>

  <h2>Why runway is the number that determines urgency</h2>
  <p>Revenue growth and product progress matter, but runway — literally how many months until the money runs out — is the number that determines how much time you actually have to reach profitability or the next fundraise, making it one of the most important numbers for any early-stage company to track.</p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter current cash in bank.</li>
    <li>Enter monthly revenue and monthly expenses.</li>
    <li>See your monthly burn rate and remaining runway in months.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>$500,000 in the bank, $20,000 monthly revenue, and $60,000 monthly expenses gives a $40,000/month net burn rate — roughly 12.5 months of runway at current burn.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using average burn rate</strong> when expenses are actually growing month over month, understating how quickly runway is actually shrinking.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting one-time expenses</strong> (annual software licenses, equipment purchases) that don't show up in a simple monthly average.</li>
    <li><strong>Not revisiting runway calculations regularly</strong> as revenue and expenses change, relying on a stale number for planning decisions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Tracking how much time remains before needing to raise funding or reach profitability</li>
    <li>Communicating financial runway clearly to a board or investors</li>
    <li>Deciding whether current spending needs to be cut to extend runway</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>How do you calculate SaaS runway?</strong> Divide current cash in the bank by your monthly net burn (expenses minus revenue). The result is the number of months you can operate before running out of cash at the current rate.</p>
  <p><strong>Should I use gross burn or net burn for runway?</strong> Always net burn — gross burn ignores incoming revenue and will understate how much runway you actually have, especially as a company starts generating meaningful revenue.</p>
  <p><strong>How often should I recalculate runway?</strong> Monthly at minimum — burn rate can shift meaningfully as revenue and expenses change, and stale runway numbers can lead to poor timing on fundraising or cost-cutting decisions.</p>
</ToolLayout>
